@page "/components/collapse"

<PageOutlet Url="components/collapse"
            Title="Collapse"
            Description="The Collapse component shows and hides a section of related content with an animated transition, leaving the trigger that toggles it to the page around it." />

<DemoPage Name="Collapse"
          SecondaryNames="@(["Collapsible", "Disclosure"])"
          Description="Collapse opens and closes a section of a page. It renders no trigger of its own, so anything can drive it, and it animates the real size of its content - downwards or sideways, all the way or down to a peek. Closed content leaves the tab order and the accessibility tree yet stays reachable by find-in-page, and expensive content can stay out of the DOM until it is opened."
          Parameters="componentParameters"
          PublicMembers="componentPublicMembers"
          SubEnums="componentSubEnums"
          SubClasses="componentSubClasses"
          CssVariables="componentCssVariables"
          GitHubUrl="Surfaces/Collapse/BitCollapse.razor"
          GitHubDemoUrl="Surfaces/Collapse/BitCollapseDemo.razor">
    <NotesTemplate>
        <BitText>
            The transition honors the reduced motion preference of the OS/browser (prefers-reduced-motion) and finishes at
            once, whatever <b>Duration</b> says. If nothing on this page animates, turn that setting off, or use the
            ForceAnimation parameter or the ForceAnimation toggle at the top of this page.
        </BitText>
    </NotesTemplate>

    <Examples>
        <DemoExample Title="Basic" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example1CsharpCode" Id="example1">
            <div>
                <b>Expanded</b> opens and closes the section. The collapse draws no trigger, so whatever flips the value lives
                on the page beside it, and the size follows the content, however late it loads or wraps.
            </div>
            <br /><br />
            <div>
                <BitToggleButton OnText="Collapse" OffText="Expand" @bind-IsChecked="expanded" />
                <BitCollapse Expanded="expanded">
                    In the beginning, there is silence a blank canvas yearning to be filled, a quiet space where creativity waits
                    to awaken. These words are temporary, standing in place of ideas yet to come, a glimpse into the infinite
                    possibilities that lie ahead. Think of this text as a bridge, connecting the empty spaces of now with the
                    vibrant narratives of tomorrow. It whispers of the stories waiting to be told, of the thoughts yet to be
                    shaped into meaning, and the emotions ready to resonate with every reader.
                </BitCollapse>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Binding & control" RazorCode="@example2RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example2CsharpCode" Id="example2">
            <div>
                <b>@@bind-Expanded</b> keeps the state on the page, <b>DefaultExpanded</b> leaves it to the component, and
                <b>ExpandAsync</b>, <b>CollapseAsync</b> and <b>ToggleAsync</b> drive it through a reference. <b>OnChange</b>
                reports the changes the component makes itself. A disabled collapse (<b>Disabled</b>) keeps its state,
                ignores the three methods and takes the disabled colors.
            </div>
            <br /><br />
            <BitToggleButton OnText="Collapse" OffText="Expand" @bind-IsChecked="boundExpanded" />
            <BitCollapse @bind-Expanded="boundExpanded">
                The button and the collapse share one value: <b>@boundExpanded</b>.
            </BitCollapse>
            <br /><br />
            <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:0.5rem">
                <BitButton OnClick="() => collapseRef?.ExpandAsync()">Expand</BitButton>
                <BitButton OnClick="() => collapseRef?.CollapseAsync()">Collapse</BitButton>
                <BitButton OnClick="() => collapseRef?.ToggleAsync()">Toggle</BitButton>
                <BitCheckbox Label="Enabled" @bind-Value="collapseEnabled" />
            </div>
            <BitCollapse @ref="collapseRef" DefaultExpanded Disabled="collapseEnabled is false" OnChange="HandleChange">
                Starts open through DefaultExpanded; nothing on the page holds its state.
            </BitCollapse>
            <div>@changeLog</div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Padding & background" RazorCode="@example3RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example3CsharpCode" Id="example3">
            <div>
                The collapse pads its content and paints the primary background. <b>NoPadding</b> removes the padding for content
                with insets of its own, and <b>Background</b> picks another color kind, <b>Transparent</b> included.
            </div>
            <br /><br />
            <BitToggleButton OnText="Collapse" OffText="Expand" @bind-IsChecked="surfaceExpanded" />
            <br /><br />
            <BitCollapse Expanded="surfaceExpanded">Default: padding and the primary background.</BitCollapse>
            <br />
            <BitCollapse Expanded="surfaceExpanded" Background="BitColorKind.Secondary">Secondary background.</BitCollapse>
            <br />
            <BitCollapse Expanded="surfaceExpanded" Background="BitColorKind.Transparent" NoPadding>
                <BitMessage Color="BitColor.Info">Transparent, no padding: the content carries its own surface and insets.</BitMessage>
            </BitCollapse>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Horizontal" RazorCode="@example4RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example4CsharpCode" Id="example4">
            <div>
                <b>Horizontal</b> opens the section sideways from the start edge, at the width of its content - a collapsing
                sidebar, a search box growing out of its icon, a row of toolbar actions.
            </div>
            <br /><br />
            <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:1rem">
                <BitToggleButton OnText="Collapse" OffText="Expand" @bind-IsChecked="horizontalExpanded" />
                <BitCollapse Horizontal Expanded="horizontalExpanded" Background="BitColorKind.Secondary">
                    <div style="white-space:nowrap">This panel opens sideways.</div>
                </BitCollapse>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Collapsed size" RazorCode="@example5RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example5CsharpCode" Id="example5">
            <div>
                <b>CollapsedSize</b> (any CSS length, padding included) leaves a peek of the content on the page - a "show more"
                clamp. A peek keeps its opacity and stays readable by assistive technology; on a Horizontal collapse it is a width.
            </div>
            <br /><br />
            <BitCollapse Expanded="peekExpanded" CollapsedSize="4.5rem">
                In the beginning, there is silence a blank canvas yearning to be filled, a quiet space where creativity waits
                to awaken. These words are temporary, standing in place of ideas yet to come, a glimpse into the infinite
                possibilities that lie ahead. Think of this text as a bridge, connecting the empty spaces of now with the
                vibrant narratives of tomorrow. It whispers of the stories waiting to be told, of the thoughts yet to be
                shaped into meaning, and the emotions ready to resonate with every reader.
                <br />
                In this space, potential reigns supreme. It is a moment suspended in time, where imagination dances freely and
                each word has the power to transform into something extraordinary. Here lies the start of something new-an
                opportunity to craft, inspire, and create.
            </BitCollapse>
            <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Text" OnClick="() => peekExpanded = !peekExpanded">
                @(peekExpanded ? "Show less" : "Show more")
            </BitButton>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Transition" RazorCode="@example6RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example6CsharpCode" Id="example6">
            <div>
                <b>Duration</b>, <b>Delay</b> (ms) and <b>Easing</b> (any CSS timing function) retune the transition;
                <b>ExpandDuration</b> and <b>CollapseDuration</b> retune one direction. <b>NoFade</b> leaves the size to do the
                work alone, and <b>NoAnimation</b> removes the transition.
            </div>
            <br /><br />
            <BitToggleButton OnText="Collapse" OffText="Expand" @bind-IsChecked="transitionExpanded" />
            <br /><br />
            <div>Duration="1000" Delay="200" and an overshooting Easing</div>
            <BitCollapse Expanded="transitionExpanded" Duration="1000" Delay="200" Easing="cubic-bezier(0.68, -0.55, 0.27, 1.55)">
                A thousand milliseconds after a two hundred millisecond wait.
            </BitCollapse>
            <br />
            <div>ExpandDuration="900" CollapseDuration="200"</div>
            <BitCollapse Expanded="transitionExpanded" ExpandDuration="900" CollapseDuration="200">
                Opens slowly, closes quickly.
            </BitCollapse>
            <br />
            <div>NoFade</div>
            <BitCollapse Expanded="transitionExpanded" NoFade Duration="800">
                The content never changes its opacity.
            </BitCollapse>
            <br />
            <div>NoAnimation</div>
            <BitCollapse Expanded="transitionExpanded" NoAnimation>
                Appears and disappears at once.
            </BitCollapse>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Transition events" RazorCode="@example7RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example7CsharpCode" Id="example7">
            <div>
                <b>OnExpanding</b> / <b>OnCollapsing</b> fire as the transition starts, <b>OnExpanded</b> / <b>OnCollapsed</b>
                when the transition the browser actually played has ended - whatever set its pace - which is the moment to
                scroll to, measure or focus the section. All four report every change; a collapse's initial state reports none.
            </div>
            <br /><br />
            <BitButton OnClick="() => eventsCollapseRef?.ToggleAsync()">@(eventsExpanded ? "Collapse" : "Expand")</BitButton>
            <BitCollapse @ref="eventsCollapseRef"
                         @bind-Expanded="eventsExpanded"
                         Duration="600"
                         OnChange="HandleEventsChange"
                         OnExpanding="HandleEventsExpanding"
                         OnCollapsing="HandleEventsCollapsing"
                         OnExpanded="HandleEventsExpanded"
                         OnCollapsed="HandleEventsCollapsed">
                The -ing callback lands at once; the -ed one six hundred milliseconds later.
            </BitCollapse>
            <br />
            <div>
                @foreach (var entry in eventsLog)
                {
                    <div>@entry</div>
                }
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Overflow" RazorCode="@example8RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example8CsharpCode" Id="example8">
            <div>
                The collapse clips its content so the transition reads as a section opening. <b>NoClip</b> stops clipping once
                it has finished opening, so a focus ring, a shadow or a dropdown menu inside it is drawn in full.
            </div>
            <br /><br />
            <BitToggleButton OnText="Collapse" OffText="Expand" @bind-IsChecked="clipExpanded" />
            <br /><br />
            <div>
                <BitCollapse Expanded="clipExpanded" NoPadding Duration="600">
                    <div class="clip-card">Default: the glow is cut off at the edge.</div>
                </BitCollapse>
                <br /><br />
                <BitCollapse Expanded="clipExpanded" NoPadding NoClip Duration="600">
                    <div class="clip-card">NoClip: the glow is drawn in full once the section is open.</div>
                </BitCollapse>
                <br />
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Rendering & printing" RazorCode="@example9RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example9CsharpCode" Id="example9">
            <div>
                <b>LazyRender</b> builds the content on the first open, and <b>UnmountOnCollapse</b> drops it once the close has
                played - losing whatever it held. <b>ExpandOnPrint</b> prints a closed section open (try print preview) and keeps
                its content in the DOM for it.
            </div>
            <br /><br />
            <BitToggleButton OnText="Collapse" OffText="Expand" @bind-IsChecked="renderingExpanded" />
            <br /><br />
            <div>LazyRender</div>
            <BitCollapse LazyRender Expanded="renderingExpanded" OnExpanded="() => lazyOpenCount++">
                Built on the first open, kept since. Opened @lazyOpenCount time(s).
            </BitCollapse>
            <br />
            <div>UnmountOnCollapse</div>
            <BitCollapse UnmountOnCollapse Expanded="renderingExpanded">
                <BitTextField Label="Type something, then close and reopen" />
            </BitCollapse>
            <br />
            <div>ExpandOnPrint</div>
            <BitCollapse ExpandOnPrint Expanded="renderingExpanded">
                On paper this section is always open.
            </BitCollapse>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Find in page" RazorCode="@example10RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example10CsharpCode" Id="example10">
            <div>
                <b>HiddenUntilFound</b> lets find-in-page and fragment links reach into a closed section: the browser opens it
                around the match and the change is reported like any other. Its content stays in the DOM; a collapse that cannot
                open (disabled, or a one-way <b>Expanded</b>) is not offered to the search.
            </div>
            <br /><br />
            <BitToggleButton OnText="Collapse" OffText="Expand" @bind-IsChecked="findExpanded" />
            <BitCollapse HiddenUntilFound @bind-Expanded="findExpanded">
                The passphrase kept in this section is <b>marmalade skies</b>. Close the section, press Ctrl+F and search for it.
            </BitCollapse>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Accessibility" RazorCode="@example11RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example11CsharpCode" Id="example11">
            <div>
                Closed content leaves the tab order and the accessibility tree. The trigger is yours: a button with a fixed
                label, <b>aria-expanded</b> and <b>aria-controls</b> set to <b>ContentId</b> (the root's id + <b>-content</b>),
                which <b>BitToggleButton</b> renders through <b>AriaMode="Expanded"</b> and <b>AriaControls</b>.
                <b>LabelledBy</b> or <b>AriaLabel</b> names the <b>region</b>; an empty <b>Role</b> drops the role and the name.
                An open region is a tab stop (<b>TabIndex="-1"</b> takes it out) and <b>FocusAsync</b> moves the focus into
                it, best from <b>OnExpanded</b>. A section closed from inside hands the focus back to its trigger from <b>OnCollapsing</b>.
            </div>
            <br /><br />
            <BitToggleButton Id="a11y-trigger"
                             Text="Shipping details"
                             AriaMode="BitToggleButtonAriaMode.Expanded"
                             AriaControls="a11y-collapse-content"
                             @bind-IsChecked="a11yExpanded" />
            <BitCollapse Id="a11y-collapse" Expanded="a11yExpanded" LabelledBy="a11y-trigger">
                Orders placed before 2 pm ship the same day. <BitLink Href="/components/collapse">Read the full policy</BitLink>.
            </BitCollapse>
            <br /><br />
            <BitButton @ref="focusTriggerRef" OnClick="() => focusCollapseRef?.ExpandAsync()">Open and focus</BitButton>
            <BitCollapse @ref="focusCollapseRef"
                         @bind-Expanded="focusExpanded"
                         AriaLabel="Focused section"
                         OnExpanded="HandleFocusExpanded"
                         OnCollapsing="HandleFocusCollapsing">
                FocusAsync put the focus here at the end of the expand transition.
                <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Text" OnClick="() => focusCollapseRef?.CollapseAsync()">Close</BitButton>
            </BitCollapse>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Cascading parameters" RazorCode="@example12RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example12CsharpCode" Id="example12">
            <div>
                <b>BitParams</b> with a <b>BitCollapseParams</b> sets defaults for every collapse under it; a collapse's own value
                wins (the last one keeps its padding). The state, the content, the callbacks and <b>LabelledBy</b> are not cascaded.
            </div>
            <br /><br />
            <BitToggleButton OnText="Collapse" OffText="Expand" @bind-IsChecked="cascadingExpanded" />
            <br /><br />
            <BitParams Parameters="collapseParams">
                <BitCollapse Expanded="cascadingExpanded">Secondary background, no padding, a slower pace.</BitCollapse>
                <br />
                <BitCollapse Expanded="cascadingExpanded">The same, from the same BitParams.</BitCollapse>
                <br />
                <BitCollapse Expanded="cascadingExpanded" NoPadding="false">Keeps its own padding.</BitCollapse>
            </BitParams>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Style & Class" RazorCode="@example13RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example13CsharpCode" Id="example13">
            <div>
                <b>Style</b> and <b>Class</b> land on the root; <b>Styles</b> and <b>Classes</b> reach its parts: <b>Root</b>,
                the <b>Expanded</b> / <b>Collapsed</b> states of the root, the <b>Content</b> region and the padded <b>Wrapper</b>.
            </div>
            <br /><br />
            <BitToggleButton OnText="Collapse" OffText="Expand" @bind-IsChecked="styleExpanded" />
            <br /><br />
            <div>
                <BitCollapse Expanded="styleExpanded" Style="border-inline-start: 4px solid var(--bit-clr-pri);">
                    A styled root.
                </BitCollapse>
                <br />
                <BitCollapse Expanded="styleExpanded"
                             Styles="@(new() { Expanded = "border: 1px solid var(--bit-clr-brd-pri);", Wrapper = "font-style: italic;" })">
                    Styles for the expanded root and the wrapper.
                </BitCollapse>
                <br />
                <BitCollapse Expanded="styleExpanded" Classes="@(new() { Expanded = "custom-expanded", Wrapper = "custom-wrapper" })">
                    Classes for the expanded root and the wrapper.
                </BitCollapse>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="RTL" RazorCode="@example14RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example14CsharpCode" Id="example14">
            <div>
                A vertical collapse is unchanged by the direction; a horizontal one opens from the start edge, the right in RTL.
            </div>
            <br /><br />
            <div dir="rtl">
                <BitToggleButton OnText="بستن" OffText="باز کردن" @bind-IsChecked="rtlExpanded" />
                <BitCollapse Expanded="rtlExpanded" Dir="BitDir.Rtl">
                    لورم ایپسوم متن ساختگی با تولید سادگی نامفهوم از صنعت چاپ و با استفاده از طراحان گرافیک است.
                    چاپگرها و متون بلکه روزنامه و مجله در ستون و سطرآنچنان که لازم است
                    و برای شرایط فعلی تکنولوژی مورد نیاز و کاربردهای متنوع با هدف بهبود ابزارهای کاربردی می باشد.
                </BitCollapse>
                <br />
                <BitCollapse Horizontal Expanded="rtlExpanded" Dir="BitDir.Rtl" Background="BitColorKind.Secondary">
                    <div style="white-space:nowrap">این بخش از سمت راست باز می شود.</div>
                </BitCollapse>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>
    </Examples>
</DemoPage>
